---
/**
 * Four cheap models, measured — and the same line extended with a ruler to a
 * size nobody had built yet.
 *
 * The teaching load is carried by the horizontal axis, whose ticks MULTIPLY
 * rather than add, and by the split between solid and dashed. Everything left
 * of the fourth dot is evidence; everything right of it is arithmetic. That
 * distinction is the whole reason the money moved, so it is drawn three ways
 * over — solid line against dashed line, filled circle against hollow diamond,
 * and a label on each half — rather than by colour (hard rule 9).
 *
 * The floor is drawn because the honest curve does not head for zero. Some of
 * what comes next in a document is genuinely unguessable, and the unit says so
 * in the paragraph beside this figure.
 *
 * Decorative on its own, so `aria-hidden`: `Figure` supplies the accessible
 * name and the description.
 */
---

<svg viewBox="0 0 640 316" class="w-full" aria-hidden="true">
  <rect
    x="8"
    y="8"
    width="624"
    height="300"
    rx="8"
    class="fill-none stroke-rule-strong"
    stroke-width="1.25"></rect>

  <text
    x="24"
    y="32"
    class="fill-ink font-display"
    font-size="14"
    font-weight="700">Four small models, and a line read forwards</text
  >
  <text x="24" y="48" class="fill-ink-faint font-mono" font-size="9"
    >every step along the bottom is ten times the size, the text and the
    computing of the one before</text
  >

  <!-- Axes. The bottom one multiplies; that is the point of the whole figure. -->
  <text x="64" y="62" class="fill-ink-faint font-mono" font-size="9"
    >how wrong it is — higher is worse</text
  >
  <g class="stroke-rule" stroke-width="1">
    <line x1="64" y1="66" x2="64" y2="240"></line>
    <line x1="64" y1="240" x2="616" y2="240"></line>
  </g>

  <!-- Measured: four models that were actually built. -->
  <polyline
    points="80,78 184,98 288,118 392,138"
    class="fill-none stroke-accent-2"
    stroke-width="2"></polyline>

  <!-- Predicted: the same line, extended over ground nobody had walked on. -->
  <polyline
    points="392,138 600,178"
    class="fill-none stroke-accent-2"
    stroke-width="2"
    stroke-dasharray="6 4"></polyline>

  <g class="fill-ink">
    <circle cx="80" cy="78" r="4"></circle>
    <circle cx="184" cy="98" r="4"></circle>
    <circle cx="288" cy="118" r="4"></circle>
    <circle cx="392" cy="138" r="4"></circle>
  </g>

  <!-- The forecast, and the machine that later landed inside it. -->
  <polygon
    points="600,168 610,178 600,188 590,178"
    class="fill-paper stroke-accent"
    stroke-width="1.5"></polygon>
  <circle cx="600" cy="178" r="3" class="fill-ink"></circle>

  <g class="fill-ink-muted font-mono" font-size="8.5">
    <text x="92" y="150">four small models, actually built</text>
    <text x="92" y="162">and measured — the cheap part</text>
    <text x="404" y="112">nothing had ever been built out here</text>
    <text x="404" y="124">so this half of the line was a prediction</text>
    <text x="616" y="200" text-anchor="end"
      >predicted from the four dots on the left</text
    >
    <text x="616" y="212" text-anchor="end">then built, and it landed here</text
    >
  </g>

  <!-- The curve is heading somewhere, and that somewhere is not zero. -->
  <line
    x1="64"
    y1="222"
    x2="616"
    y2="222"
    class="stroke-rule-strong"
    stroke-width="1"
    stroke-dasharray="3 3"></line>
  <text x="72" y="234" class="fill-ink-faint font-mono" font-size="8"
    >the floor — some of what comes next is genuinely unguessable</text
  >

  <g class="fill-ink-muted font-mono" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle">
    <text x="80" y="254">×1</text>
    <text x="184" y="254">×10</text>
    <text x="288" y="254">×100</text>
    <text x="392" y="254">×1,000</text>
    <text x="496" y="254">×10,000</text>
    <text x="600" y="254">×100,000</text>
  </g>
  <text x="64" y="270" class="fill-ink-faint font-mono" font-size="9"
    >how big — model, text and computing, all multiplied together</text
  >

  <text
    x="24"
    y="294"
    class="fill-ink font-display"
    font-size="12"
    font-weight="700"
    >Adding to it bought almost nothing. Multiplying it bought the same drop,
    every time.</text
  >
</svg>
